    "q-14954": {
      "dom_id": "step-q14954",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{6}{5\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{{n}^{6}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[n]{n}})}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot {1}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{6}{5}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
